      <li>
      Define a <code>Subscription</code> object:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1\nkind: Subscription\nmetadata:\n  name: compliance-operator-sub\n  namespace: openshift-compliance\nspec:\n  channel: \"stable\"\n  installPlanApproval: Automatic\n  name: compliance-operator\n  source: redhat-operators\n  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace\n  config:\n    nodeSelector:\n      node-role.kubernetes.io/worker: \"\""} lang="yaml" title="Example subscription-object.yaml file" />
      <ul><li>Update the Operator deployment to deploy on <code>worker</code> nodes.</li></ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>Subscription</code> object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f subscription-object.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify that the installation succeeded by running the following command to inspect the cluster service version (CSV) file:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get csv -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the Compliance Operator is up and running by using the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get deploy -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <h2 id="installing-compliance-operator-hcp_compliance-operator-installation" class="group scroll-mt-20">Installing the Compliance Operator on hosted control planes<a href="#installing-compliance-operator-hcp_compliance-operator-installation" class="ml-2 text-muted-foreground opacity-0 group-hover:opacity-100 no-underline" aria-hidden="true" tabindex="-1">&para;</a></h2>
      <p class="_abstract">Install the Compliance Operator on hosted control planes by creating a <code>Subscription</code> file in the software catalog so you can run compliance scans in a hosted control plane environment.</p>
      <Alert variant="info" class="my-4">
        <AlertTitle>Important</AlertTitle>
        <AlertDescription>
          <p>Hosted control planes is a Technology Preview feature only. Technology Preview features are not supported with Red Hat production service level agreements (SLAs) and might not be functionally complete. Red Hat does not recommend using them in production. These features provide early access to upcoming product features, enabling customers to test functionality and provide feedback during the development process.</p>
          <p>For more information about the support scope of Red Hat Technology Preview features, see <a href="https://access.redhat.com/support/offerings/techpreview/">Technology Preview Features Support Scope</a>.</p>
        </AlertDescription>
      </Alert>
      <p><strong>Prerequisites</strong></p>
      <ul><li>You must have <code>admin</code> privileges.</li></ul>
      <p><strong>Procedure</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Define a <code>Namespace</code> object similar to the following:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: v1\nkind: Namespace\nmetadata:\n  labels:\n    openshift.io/cluster-monitoring: \"true\"\n    pod-security.kubernetes.io/enforce: privileged\n  name: openshift-compliance"} lang="yaml" title="Example namespace-object.yaml" />
      <ul><li>In OpenShift Container Platform 4.22, the pod security label must be set to <code>privileged</code> at the namespace level.</li></ul>
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>Namespace</code> object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f namespace-object.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Define an <code>OperatorGroup</code> object:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1\nkind: OperatorGroup\nmetadata:\n  name: compliance-operator\n  namespace: openshift-compliance\nspec:\n  targetNamespaces:\n  - openshift-compliance"} lang="yaml" title="Example operator-group-object.yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>OperatorGroup</code> object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f operator-group-object.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Define a <code>Subscription</code> object:
      <CodeBlock code={"apiVersion: operators.coreos.com/v1alpha1\nkind: Subscription\nmetadata:\n  name: compliance-operator-sub\n  namespace: openshift-compliance\nspec:\n  channel: \"stable\"\n  installPlanApproval: Automatic\n  name: compliance-operator\n  source: redhat-operators\n  sourceNamespace: openshift-marketplace\n  config:\n    nodeSelector:\n      node-role.kubernetes.io/worker: \"\"\n    env:\n    - name: PLATFORM\n      value: \"HyperShift\""} lang="yaml" title="Example subscription-object.yaml" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Create the <code>Subscription</code> object by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc create -f subscription-object.yaml"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
      <p><strong>Verification</strong></p>
      <ol>
      <li>
      Verify the installation succeeded by inspecting the CSV file by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get csv -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      <li>
      Verify that the Compliance Operator is up and running by running the following command:
      <CodeBlock code={"$ oc get deploy -n openshift-compliance"} lang="terminal" />
      </li>
      </ol>
